---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Carousel from '../components/Carousel.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const copy = 'body-copy space-y-6 text-lg/8 tracking-[-0.01em] text-pretty *:max-w-[60ch]';
---

<Layout title="Ambotics" description="A new robotics company. Coming soon.">
  <main id="top" class="isolate">
    <Hero />

    <!-- The four paragraphs are one continuous argument — ¶3 opens "So the
         question isn't really…" — so they sit under a single heading rather than
         being split at a seam they do not have. -->
    <Section id="starting" heading="Robots are changing">
      <div class={copy}>
        <p>
          Robots aren't new. They've been welding and painting in factories since the 1960s
          and they're very good at it. The catch was always the conditions around them. A
          conventional robot needs the world held still: parts in fixed positions, a cage, a
          program written for exactly one task. Change the task and you pay for the
          integration again. That's why robots filled car plants and stayed rare almost
          everywhere else.
        </p>
        <p>
          AI is what's changing them. A robot can now see what's actually in front of it,
          handle parts that arrive at odd angles, and pick up a new task by being shown it
          rather than reprogrammed. That moves the line of what's worth automating. Small
          batches, frequent changeovers and jobs that vary a little every time are all within
          reach now.
        </p>
        <p>
          So the question isn't really whether a machine can do the work. It's whether doing
          it that way pays. That's a business question, and most companies in this field
          aren't built to answer it — they lead with the hardware and talk to other
          engineers. We'd rather start with your operation and what the work actually costs
          you. If automating a task won't produce a real return, we'll tell you and leave it
          alone.
        </p>
        <p>
          We build the whole stack ourselves: software, hardware, data, training and
          evaluation. That means we can change things quickly instead of waiting on a
          supplier. And when we take a job on, we take all of it — the hardware, the
          installation, the daily running and the support long after the first day. You
          shouldn't have to become a robotics expert or line up a row of vendors to keep it
          working.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Section>

    <Section id="applications" heading="Applications">
      <div class={copy}>
        <p>
          Our team has worked with IKEA, ABB, AstraZeneca and Universal Robots, in retail
          distribution, industrial robotics and pharmaceutical production. Different
          operations, but the same question comes up in each. Which repetitive work is worth
          handing to a machine?
        </p>
        <p>
          Most conversations start in the same place. Someone suspects a part of their
          operation could run better but doesn't know what's realistic or where to begin.
          That's fair enough. This has moved quickly, and there's no easy way to tell from
          the outside what's possible now.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tell us where the repetitive work is and we'll give you a straight answer on
          whether it's worth automating. If it is, we'll build it, run it and stand behind
          it.
        </p>
      </div>

      <Carousel slot="wide" />
    </Section>

    <Section id="contact" heading="Get in touch">
      <ContactForm />
    </Section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Layout>
